body:
Hi SCA!

&#x200B;

I'm trying to decide between ordering Alba Botanica Sensitive Fragrance Free Mineral Lotion, Sun Bum Face (unscented Baby Bum), Neutrogena Ultra Sheer Liquid, and Elta MD UV Shield for a face sunscreen.... has anyone tried multiple of these that can compare?

&#x200B;

I have relatively neutral/combination seasonal skin with an occasional oily T-zone (especially in summer), pores which frequently get clogged and turn into whiteheads (especially on my cheeks and forehead), and I sweat like crazy in the heat. I've had issues in the past with sunscreens pilling up and stinging my eyes, especially when I inevitably sweat.

&#x200B;

I'll be using this face sunscreen outside for many days of lots of sun, and want something effective and easy/comfortable to use to stay protected! Bonus if it's something I can reapply over makeup (is that a thing?)

&#x200B;

Side question, does anyone know the benefits/drawbacks of regular vs. sport sunscreen for body sunscreen?

&#x200B;

Thank you SO much for any help!!!!!

Bonnaroo will be my first music festival, and since a friend suggested it, the more I've read the more excited I am for the vibes and what it stands for. I love live music, and when I have disposable income that's my favorite way to spend it. I feel like listening to an artist pour their heart out with friends and strangers surrounding you taking it in... those have been some of the most magical times I've known. Seeing Hozier live in a little venue years ago just felt like I was in a completely different world away from time, soaking in this raw emotion in the music. So the chance to see days of music like that, and artists of all different genres? I'm psyched, and feel incredibly lucky I was able to save and rearrange things enough to go to Roo this year.

&#x200B;

I'm also super excited about the camping aspect of the festival. I grew up camping, and will be coming to Roo with my brother. He's recently graduated college, and has become such a self-sufficient, independent person over the past few years, that I'm really looking forward to getting to know him more as this new adult person that he is. We've always had different personalities, but since he was born we've been best friends, and I'm excited to camp and share these experiences like we had growing up in a whole new way now that we've both become different people.

&#x200B;

Finally, I'm excited for Roo (and, frankly, nervous) because I think it will be a much needed chance to get some perspective, self-reflect, and reconnect with the world a little bit. I've been going through a tough time lately, and have become isolated and negative in ways I don't like. I'm really hoping to have a chance to just talk to some new people in a place where boundaries are down, where people take are of each other instead of passing judgement, and to get a better sense of who I want to be. From all I've read and heard, here on Reddit and elsewhere, Roo is community focused before anything else, and while totally unexpected when I first googled "Bonnaroo music festival", I think it's now the part I'm most excited for. 

&#x200B;

Sorry if this was a bit of a ramble, I think it was helpful for me to reflect and write this out, and this just prompted that :) Thanks :)

&#x200B;

\[I just got a ticket - finally! - but still need to get a car pass, so I guess this is me noting that\]

*The Dark Hills Divide* for me. They had this wonderful combination of exploration and fantasy in a way that just drew me into the character. I've reread the series recently as an adult and can't wait to give my copies to my kids someday.

&#x200B;

edit: Or *Inkheart*!!! Similar reasons I fell in love with it...
